findings.body:
**Critical security regression:** password-reset tokens are no longer cryptographically secure.

`Math.random()` is not a CSPRNG, and `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12)` yields at most 10¹² distinct values (~40 bits). After `.toString(36)` and `padStart(9, "0")` the token is only ~9 characters — trivially brute-forceable on a reset-confirm endpoint compared to the previous `randomBytes(32)` (256 bits / 64 hex chars).

Edge runtimes expose the Web Crypto API; use that instead of `node:crypto` or `Math.random`:

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```

Do not ship reset/session tokens derived from `Math.random`.
